I remember when I read "Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas" I loved it. 
When they finally made it into a movie they were very faithfull to the
book.  So I hated it.  Much was because I was then in a later phase of
my life and I was no longer sympathetic with the material, but some of
it was this effect.

> I think this is why very few players who play roguelikes without tiles
> like the tiles versions.  Even though the tiles are tiny, you still
> get some of the same effect.

If I want to play a graphical game there are unlimited dungeon running
first person shooter games out there.  They are all descended from rogue
in some fashion.

If I don't want to play such a graphical game, why bother going part  of
the way there?  It doesn't bother me it just seems pointless to add
graphics without going the rest of the way.

And yet ...  I have been playing roguelikes for a very long time.  Much
longer than any graphical game.  Because like chess, go, poker, bridge
or monopoly, it's not about the graphics.  With a game that's not about
the graphics, what does a small amount of graphics really add?  With
hand handle chess games it didn't add enough for me to care.


